e sharing these these issues  i think they are all the right issues  and we should be working together on  them  you uh  as a government are basically saying  that you have to stop these boats  if you can't be sure who's in these in  these boats um  what is the actual policy here do you  want to stop these boats crossing or is  it uh  the case that what you want to do is to  make sure that people are safe i mean  what what's the actual policy that  you're adopting because it doesn't seem  to be clear  what you're really trying to do here i  think our policy is very clear and that  is wha these boats must stop the people  smugglers are taking advantage of these  very vulnerable people from poor parts  of the world misleading them sticking  them in these you know the sometimes you  can't even call them boats and putting  their lives in danger this is a criminal  enterprise and for that reason it must  stop now of course we can't just do it  on our own we do need the cooperation of  the french and again you asked me about  the pm's letter and i think what the pm  has set out those are exactly the kind  of things that we need to do and i hope  the the french will will take that and  work with us  but if if the point is to save these  people's lives why isn't the government  focusing more on  more legitimate routes for refugees  rather than uh putting people in a  situation where okay they may be legal  but they're going to do this shouldn't  shouldn't you be focusing on making sure  that they have more legitimate routes by  which they can prove or not prove their  asylum status and so great question and  that is exactly what the government does  in fact the uk should be very proud of  the sanctions  actually since 2015 i think we have  resettled the uk through um through the  refugee programs we've resettled over 25  000 people more than any other country  in europe when i was home secretary i  expanded those programs uh pretty patel  has also expanded those programs we  should be very proud on the on the big  offer we make to people that are in  conflict zones and elsewhere uh to to  come to safety just as we've done in the  last few months with with afghan  arrivals is absolutely the right thing  to do the wrong thing to do is to let  these small boats and let these criminal  enterprises your profit from these  people and and put these lives in danger  we will never tolerate that and it is  right that we do everything we can to  prevent it i understand the words but  let's look at the numbers  they are going up and up and up since  you are home secretary they've gone from  a couple of thousand a year to  eight to ten thousand a year and now  this year it'll be upwards of 25 000.  whatever you say you're doing the  reality is more and more people are  coming uh coming in through this route  risking their lives as well as uh  creating illegal landings you haven't  got enough offshore base uh through  which to process them you haven't got a  relationship with the french i mean  pretty patel doesn't even get invited to  meetings anymore the french won't have  them back  what is your plan to solve this well i  think that the home secretary is doing a  lot so for example the the as you just  said she it's a thing we have to do in  cooperation with others and she doesn't  even get involved invited to the  discussion to solve it  part of it of course is about  cooperation and that is a big important  part of it part of it is things that we  can do domestically so the nationality  and borders bill that we have in front  of parliament now uh will make this  you're dealing with this challenge uh  you're easier than it is today it will  mean that you know for example that if  you are if you are someone that comes  from a safe country like france and then  somehow gets to the uk and then claims  asylum that we will you first understand  that you're more likely to have been an  economic migrant because otherwise you  would have claimed asylum in the first  safe country you are in and and i think  it is important to have these these  changes  in law and so these measures are being  taken forward but at the same time you  know whatever we can do we still need to  have uh cooperation with our french  friends and i think reaching out to them  and and suggesting your proactive  suggestions around whether it's joint  patrolling uh whether it's a returns  agreement i think these are all the  right things to do um look i'm not  making a stupid pun here but there is  some sense that the government is a bit  adrift at sea here this week  prime minister lost his place in a big  speech  he sung the praises of a cartoon pig  he saw his majority cut from 80 to  26 on one of your key policies social  care and he triggered a diplomatic role  with the french when we absolutely as  you've just been telling me need their  cooperation  uh do you still think boris johnson is  an election winner  yes absolutely and let me tell you why  because we are delivering on our  promises in the same of that same time  frame you just talked about we have set  out a an investment fraud of 96 billion  pounds in rail the biggest this country  has ever seen absolutely transformative  we are taken  took through parliament the biggest  reform to social care ever with the most  generous means-testing capping costs  ever that something that all governments  of the past have tried to avoid and this  government is taking on head-on  delivering on our promise and when it  comes again where we started our  conversation today when it comes to  dealing with this pandemic we have made  huge progress hospitalizations have been  falling in recent weeks we are making  huge progress we have the most  successful booster program in europe so  i think the the this is a government  that delivers on its promises and deals  with the challenges they face so to all  those grumbling conservative black  benchers who are saying oh maybe it's  time for the prime minister to uh start  his next career you're saying he's going  to be here and he will lead us into the  next election yes  health secretary thank you very much  indeed thank you
 

